<p>"Contemporary" is one of the most requested styles in Indian design briefs and one of the least clearly defined. People often mean "not traditional" or "like the photos online". This guide pins it down, then shows how to adapt it to Indian light, climate, cooking and family life so it feels like home rather than a showroom.</p>

<h2>What contemporary actually means</h2>
<p>Contemporary is the design language of the present moment. It is not a fixed rulebook: it absorbs what is current, from warm wood to curved furniture to handmade textiles, and edits it into a calm, uncluttered space. That is what separates it from its two closest relatives.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Contemporary</th><th>Modern</th><th>Minimalist</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Idea</td><td>What feels current now</td><td>A defined mid-century style</td><td>Only what is essential</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Changes over time?</td><td>Yes, continuously</td><td>No, it is historical</td><td>Rarely</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour</td><td>Warm neutrals with one or two accents</td><td>Earthy tones, teak, primary accents</td><td>White, grey, black</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decoration</td><td>A few chosen pieces</td><td>Classic design objects</td><td>Almost none</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Feels</td><td>Relaxed, layered, lived in</td><td>Retro, structured</td><td>Spare, quiet</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Six principles that hold it together</h2>
<div class="grid grid--sm">
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Line</span><h3 class="card__title">Clean profiles</h3><p class="card__text">Low, horizontal furniture, flush joinery and slim handles. Every piece has a job.</p></div>
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Colour</span><h3 class="card__title">Warm neutral base</h3><p class="card__text">Warm whites, greige and taupe carry the room. One or two accents do the talking.</p></div>
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Surface</span><h3 class="card__title">Texture over pattern</h3><p class="card__text">Linen, oak, cane, limewash and stone give depth without visual noise.</p></div>
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Light</span><h3 class="card__title">Layered lighting</h3><p class="card__text">Ambient, task and accent light together, on dimmers, instead of one bright ceiling fixture.</p></div>
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Nature</span><h3 class="card__title">Living elements</h3><p class="card__text">A large plant, natural fibres and organic shapes soften straight lines.</p></div>
  <div class="card card--boxed"><span class="card__label">Art</span><h3 class="card__title">One strong piece</h3><p class="card__text">A single large artwork or craft piece per wall, chosen with intent.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Three quick rules</span>Split colour roughly 60% dominant, 30% secondary and 10% accent. Use at least three textures in every room. Plan the lighting before you choose furniture.</div>

<h2>A colour palette that suits Indian light</h2>
<p>Cool grey schemes from northern Europe can look flat and chilly under strong Indian sun and warm-white bulbs. Warm undertones work better with our daylight, with wood and brass, and with the crafts most families already own. A starting palette:</p>
<div class="swatches">
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#F3EEE6"><b>Warm ivory</b><span>Walls and ceilings</span></div>
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#E4D9CB"><b>Oat linen</b><span>Upholstery, curtains</span></div>
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#B9A58A"><b>Greige clay</b><span>Feature wall, cabinetry</span></div>
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#8A6E52"><b>Teak brown</b><span>Wood tones, depth</span></div>
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#344A3F"><b>Deep forest</b><span>Accent wall or sofa</span></div>
  <div class="swatch-card" style="--c:#3E3A36"><b>Warm charcoal</b><span>Hardware, frames, contrast</span></div>
</div>
<p>Always test paint on the actual wall and look at it in the morning, afternoon and under your evening lights. The same shade can move from beige to pink to grey through the day.</p>

<h2>Bringing in Indian craft without clutter</h2>
<p>What makes Indian contemporary homes distinctive is the conversation between a clean frame and handmade objects. The frame gives calm; the craft gives warmth and a story. The balance usually comes from one clear Indian element per room.</p>
<div class="pros-cons">
  <div><span class="pros-cons__title">Bring in</span><ul><li>A hand-knotted dhurrie or kilim to anchor the living room</li><li>Brass or copper vessels as a small curated display</li><li>Cane or rattan in a chair, headboard or pendant</li><li>One Madhubani, Gond, Pichwai or Warli artwork</li><li>Block-printed textiles on cushions or a framed panel</li><li>Teak or sheesham in a console or a bench</li></ul></div>
  <div><span class="pros-cons__title">Keep contemporary</span><ul><li>Low-profile seating with simple arms</li><li>Concealed storage with flush shutters</li><li>Handle-free or slim-profile kitchen cabinets</li><li>Open plan with clear zones for sitting and dining</li><li>No more than three objects on any shelf</li><li>One consistent metal finish throughout</li></ul></div>
</div>

<h2>Room by room</h2>
<h3>Living room</h3>
<p>Pull the sofa off the wall if space allows, layer a jute rug under a softer one, and hang one large artwork rather than a gallery. Use a floor lamp and cove lighting so the ceiling downlights are not the only source. Keep the TV wall simple: a panel in wood veneer or a textured finish, with closed storage below.</p>
<h3>Bedroom</h3>
<p>A tall upholstered or panelled headboard, pendant lights instead of bedside lamps, and floor-to-ceiling curtains with a blackout lining. Hide the AC behind a bulkhead or slatted panel. Add one texture moment, such as a cane headboard or a velvet bench.</p>
<h3>Kitchen</h3>
<p>Flat shutters with integrated handles, a plain quartz or honed granite top, and a large-format tile or single-slab backsplash. Under-cabinet lights make the work zone brighter and the room calmer. Matte finishes hide the reality of daily Indian cooking better than gloss; see our <a href="/materials/acrylic-finish/">acrylic guide</a> and <a href="/blogs/kitchen-colour-combinations/">colour combinations</a>.</p>
<h3>Bathroom</h3>
<p>Large tiles with fewer grout lines, a wall-hung WC and vanity to show more floor, a recessed niche in the shower instead of wire racks, and one metal finish for all fittings. A backlit mirror gives even light for grooming.</p>
<h3>Foyer</h3>
<p>A floating shoe unit with a bench, a round or arched mirror, and one good wall light. Introduce the home's palette here so the rest of the house feels connected.</p>
<h3>Study corner</h3>
<p>A built-in desk and shelves in the wall colour so they recede, a task light aimed across the screen rather than at it, and hidden cable trays. Bookshelves double as sound absorbers on hard walls.</p>

<h2>Materials that age well in Indian conditions</h2>
